    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait what? sorry i didnt quite catch that last part. what do you mean by a goblin's fingers? i'm just trying to figure out if i need to take my car to a mechanic or if i can just fix the belt myself.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No worries, I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. By \"goblin's fingers,\" I meant that when the serpentine belt is worn out, it can cause the engine's accessories to bind and vibrate, much like a goblin's fingers might get tangled in a spider's web. It's a bit of a stretch, I know!\n\nTo answer your question, I would recommend having a professional mechanic take a look at your car. While it's possible to replace the serpentine belt yourself, it can be a bit tricky, and if you're not familiar with the process, you might end up making things worse. Plus, if there's another underlying issue causing the noise, a mechanic can help identify and fix it.\n\nIt's also worth noting that a bad serpentine belt can cause other problems, such as a loss of power steering or air conditioning, which can be more expensive to repair. So, it's often best to err on the side of caution and have a professional take a look.\n\nThat being said, if you're feeling handy and confident, you can try to diagnose the issue yourself. Just be sure to consult your owner's manual and follow proper safety procedures when working with your car",
